---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the CQRS principle and the load principle share (docs/domain.md, "The principle
// map"; Q40 · Q-traffic). Neither has a lab page yet, so the bakery explains them itself.
// Static, no island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it
// on the date in READ, and every number from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.cqrs;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://cqrs.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2010/11/cqrs_documents.pdf',
    title: 'CQRS Documents',
    by: 'Greg Young, 2010',
    for: 'a service split into a command side and a query side, with consistent transactions on the first and eventual consistency on the second, kept in step by events',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/Resequencer.html',
    title: 'Resequencer',
    by: 'Gregor Hohpe and Bobby Woolf, Enterprise Integration Patterns (2003)',
    for: 'collecting messages that arrive out of order and publishing them in order, which the CRM deliberately does not do',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/batching-retries/',
    title: 'Batching, retries and delays',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'a batch is delivered when it reaches its size or its timeout, whichever comes first; a message acknowledged on its own is not delivered again; three retries by default, then the dead-letter queue',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/reference/delivery-guarantees/',
    title: 'Delivery guarantees',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'messages are delivered at least once and occasionally more than once',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/platform/pricing/',
    title: 'Queues pricing',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Queues documentation',
    for: 'a message takes 3 operations, a write, a read and a delete, and each retry another read; 1,000,000 operations a month on Workers Paid, then $0.40 a million',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'The lab',
    by: 'the lab index on ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, the Route, Queue, Cache, Backfill and Raft Labs; the Queue and Backfill Labs show the parts, and none shows the split itself',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery takes writes in the objects that decide them and builds the CRM’s views from their events through a queue, and how simulated traffic makes the lag between the two visible."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An order is decided at once, and the CRM learns of it a moment later. This is why the bakery
      is built that way, what joins the two sides, and how the console lets a visitor watch the gap
      under load instead of hiding it.
    </p>

    <h2>The order is on the board, and not yet on the timeline</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor opens the console and presses Start traffic at 6 orders a minute. Made-up customers
      start ordering. A card appears on the kitchen board almost as soon as each order is placed. In
      the customer list, that customer’s count of orders goes up later, once the payment has landed
      and the list has looked again. Nothing failed. Every order was accepted the moment it was
      placed, and the views are catching up.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious design reads the truth at the moment it is asked. A customer’s history would ask
      every Order object that customer ever had, and the campaign stats would ask all of them, a
      request each for every page view. <code>docs/adr/0004</code> rejects that as projections
      polling their sources. The other obvious design has the Order write into the CRM as it
      commits. A Durable Object’s transaction ends at its own storage, so that is a call, not a
      write, and the Order would wait on the CRM: a slow CRM would slow every checkout. No lab page
      shows this one yet. The{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        lab’s index
      </a>{' '}
      is where the lab explains the family of techniques around it; as read on {READ} its Queue and
      Backfill Labs show the parts, and none shows the split.
    </p>

    <h2>Two sides with different needs</h2>
    <p>
      Greg Young’s <em>CQRS Documents</em> (2010) splits a service into a command side and a query
      side because they want different things: commands want consistent data and a transaction,
      while most systems can let the query side be eventually consistent. The bakery takes that
      literally. The <code>Order</code>, <code>Customer</code> and <code>CateringEvent</code>{' '}
      objects take commands and decide them. The <code>Crm</code> object keeps the views the console
      reads, the customer list, the timelines, the activity feed, the lead pipeline and the campaign
      stats, and no command ever writes them (<code>crates/domain/src/crm.rs</code>). The only thing
      between the two sides is the Queue.
    </p>

    <h2>How an event crosses</h2>
    <p>
      An Order commits its events and a copy of each in its outbox in one transaction, answers the
      visitor, and sends the outbox to the <code>bakery-events</code> Queue straight after; an{' '}
      <code>outbox</code> timer 30 seconds out sends whatever did not go (in
      <code>workers/api/src/objects/stream.rs</code>). The consumer takes batches of up to 10 with a
      batch timeout of 0, so a message goes as soon as it is there (<code>wrangler.jsonc</code>).
      Each subscriber gets the event at its own path, and a message is acknowledged only when every
      subscriber answered 2xx; otherwise it is retried, and after 3 retries it moves to the
      dead-letter queue (<code>workers/api/src/queue.rs</code>). Delivery is at least once, and
      Cloudflare’s documentation, read on {READ}, promises no order.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the CRM has to make a correct view out of events that can come twice and out of order. It
      keeps the ids it applied, so a second delivery changes nothing. For each order, catering and
      customer stream it keeps the newest <code>seq</code> it applied and ignores anything not
      newer. It does not wait for gaps, because it receives only some of each stream’s events by
      design. It is not Hohpe and Woolf’s resequencer, which buffers messages until the order is
      whole. The one case it cannot apply is a later event for an order it has never seen, such as{' '}
      <code>OrderPaid</code> ahead of <code>OrderPlaced</code>: it refuses it as an unknown
      reference, and the Queue’s retry brings it back after the placement has arrived.
    </p>

    <h2>The lag is the design</h2>
    <p>
      The write that must be right now is the order: its place, its price, its payment. The read
      that may be a moment old is the timeline. Splitting them means the checkout never waits for a
      view, and a view can be thrown away and rebuilt from the CRM’s log (the{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('backfill')}>backfill principle</a>). The cost is that the screens are behind,
      and they say so. The console’s customer list and its activity feed look again every ten
      seconds (<code>apps/web/src/components/Customers.tsx</code>, <code>Activity.tsx</code>), and
      the guide tells the visitor that the CRM learns of a change “a moment later”. While a rebuild
      runs, the CRM’s answers carry <code>Projection-State: rebuilding</code>. That header marks a
      rebuild only; no screen yet shows how many events a view is behind.
    </p>
    <p>
      The kitchen board is a projection too, fed by the same Queue. It looks faster because it is
      pushed: a socket gets a snapshot when it joins and a frame for every event that changes the
      board.
    </p>

    <h2>Load you can start, and that stops itself</h2>
    <p>
      A quiet demo has nothing to catch up on. The <code>Simulator</code> object, one per sandbox,
      places orders on its own alarm, one tick a wall-time minute, as the seed’s made-up customers
      (in
      <code>workers/api/src/objects/simulator.rs</code>). Each order goes the way a visitor’s does:
      the same idempotency key check, a place asked of the day’s <code>Schedule</code>, a payment
      through Stripe’s test mode, one in six declined. Its events carry{' '}
      <code>cause: sim:&lt;ulid&gt;</code>, so the feed tells them apart. Because it passes the same
      checks, it is refused like a visitor: a smoke run at 03:30 UTC found its slots, four hours
      ahead, falling before opening, and full slots kept it under its own rate limit, so the smoke
      now moves the business clock to the next opening before it runs (<code>docs/roadmap.md</code>
      ).
    </p>
    <p>
      It is bounded three ways. At most 6 orders a wall-time minute, the next logged{' '}
      <code>sim_rate</code>. At most 30 minutes a sandbox a UTC day, reserved in{' '}
      <code>Control</code>’s ledger, a start past them refused and logged <code>sim_cap</code>. And
      only while the console is open: a tick that has seen no socket and no heartbeat (sent every 30
      seconds) for 90 seconds stops with <code>console_closed</code>. A demo left in a tab that was
      closed cannot run up a bill on its own.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One full run.</strong> 30 minutes at the top rate is 180 orders, about 546 Queue
        operations, 243 Durable Object requests and 3,000 rows written, wipe included (from
        <code>docs/cost.md</code>, planning estimates as of 2026-11-06).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Three operations a message.</strong> A write, a read and a delete, and a read more
        for each retry (Cloudflare’s Queues pricing, read on {READ}). On a day at the 300-sandbox
        cap, <code>docs/cost.md</code> counts room for about 9 full runs in the Queue budget.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Views that are behind.</strong> Any screen built on the CRM can show a state a few
        seconds old, and a reader has to know which screens those are.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in and open <a href="/app/">the console</a>. Press Start traffic and watch cards arrive
      on the kitchen board, then watch the customer list’s Orders and Last order columns follow
      them. Close the tab, and the Simulator stops on its own at its first tick after 90 seconds.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('cqrs')}>the CQRS principle</a> or{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('load')}>load you can watch</a> on How it’s built, to the decisions that chose
      them,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0003')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0003
      </a>{' '}
      and{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0004')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0004
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
